  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the maximum magnitude outcome of each lottery and preferring the one with the more favorable (higher) probability-weighted maximum outcome. In this case, Lottery B has a higher maximum outcome (4000 vs 3000) and although its probability is lower, its weighted value (800) exceeds that of A (750). However, the individual expresses preference for Lottery A due to its 'more attractive' prize, implying a focus on A’s prize desirability despite its smaller size, possibly influenced by the slightly higher probability. The individual does not reference the largest possible outcome (4000) or compare the maximum outcomes at all. Instead, the reasoning appears to downplay probability differences and elevate the appeal of A’s prize, which contradicts the logic of the decision reason that would favor B. Therefore, the individual did not consider the relevant information (maximum magnitude outcomes weighted by probability), nor did they apply the rule of the decision reason. The justification is based on a different rationale—possibly affective or heuristic attraction to A’s prize.",
